Determine the direction of the electric field at point P in the given diagram, where two charges, +Q and -Q, are situated.The electric field (E) can be understood as the electric force per unit charge, and its direction is the direction of the force that a positive test charge would experience. 

At point P, if a positive test charge is placed:
1. The force due to the +Q charge would be repulsive, causing the force direction at P to be away from the +Q charge: \(\vec{E}_{(+Q)->P}\).
2. The force due to the -Q charge would be attractive, making the force direction at P point towards the -Q charge: \(\vec{E}_{P->(-Q)}\).

To find the net electric field direction, draw two vectors at point P with equal magnitudes representing these forces. The resultant vector, formed by adding these two vectors, will indicate the direction of the electric field at point P, as shown in the figure below:
