Nitrogen and oxygen gases are combined to form nitrogen dioxide gas. All gases are at the same temperature and pressure. If 10 L of each gas react, determine the identity and volume of the excess reactant left over?Start with the balanced chemical equation for the reaction:

N2(g) + 2O2(g) -> 2NO2(g)

Notice that there is a 1:2 mole ratio between nitrogen and oxygen. This means that, regardless of how many moles of nitrogen react, you'll need twice as many moles of oxygen for the reaction to take place.

Since all the gases are at the same pressure and temperature, you can use the ideal gas law to relate the volume of each gas to its number of moles:

PV = nRT

where P is the pressure, V is the volume, n is the number of moles, R is the ideal gas constant, and T is the temperature.

Since the pressure and temperature are the same for all the gases, you can write:

n/V = constant

This means that the mole ratio between nitrogen and oxygen is also equal to the volume ratio.

Therefore, for every 10 L of nitrogen that react, you need 20 L of oxygen. Since you only have 10 L of oxygen, all the oxygen will be consumed and you'll be left with excess nitrogen.

The reaction will only consume 5 L of nitrogen, so you'll be left with 5 L of excess nitrogen.
####
Nitrogen will be the excess reactant, and 5 L of nitrogen will be left over.
